module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Nakayama
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.RigSmooth.PerturbedPresentation

/-!
# The perturbed presentation presents an isomorphic algebra

Let `A` be a Noetherian ring, `I ⊆ A` an ideal and `R` an `I`-adically complete Noetherian
`A`-algebra (the case of interest is `R = A[x₁, …, x_r]^∧`). Let
`g = (g₁, …, g_m) : R^⊕m → R` and let `g' = (g'₁, …, g'_m) : R^⊕m → R` be a perturbation of it,
`gᵢ - g'ᵢ ∈ Iⁿ R`. Write `B = R ⧸ (g₁, …, g_m)` and `B' = R ⧸ (g'₁, …, g'_m)`.

This file contains the last paragraph of the proof of Stacks 0GAR — the part the source states
and then leaves to the reader ("the rest of the proof shows that these facts force `φ` to be an
isomorphism; we suggest the reader find their own proof of this").

The route taken here is not the source's. The source deduces injectivity of the comparison map
from `Gr_I(B) ≅ Gr_I(B')` by an ascending chain argument on the associated graded ring, which
needs `Gr_I(B)` as a *ring*. Instead the comparison map is pulled back to a perturbation `σ` of
the identity of `R` itself, and then the single equality of ideals

`(g₁, …, g_m) · σ = (g'₁, …, g'_m)`

is proved by Artin-Rees plus Nakayama: the left-hand side is contained in the right-hand side
because `σ(gᵢ)` dies in `B'`, the right-hand side is inside the left-hand side plus `IⁿR`
because `σ(gᵢ)` and `g'ᵢ` are congruent, and the Artin-Rees constant for `g'` — which is
*uniform in the perturbation*, by `artinRees_stability` — converts `IⁿR` into
`I · (g'₁, …, g'_m)`. Nakayama then closes the gap, because `IR` lies in the Jacobson radical of
the complete ring `R`. Since `σ` is an automorphism (it is congruent to the identity modulo
`IR`, and `R` is complete and separated), the equality of ideals *is* the isomorphism
`B ≃ₐ[A] B'`.

## Main results

* `Rig.PerturbedPresentation.injective_of_sub_mem` and
  `Rig.PerturbedPresentation.bijective_of_sub_mem`: an `A`-algebra endomorphism of an
  `I`-adically complete `A`-algebra which is congruent to the identity modulo `IR` is an
  automorphism.
* `Rig.PerturbedPresentation.map_range_eq_range_of_artinRees`: the equality of ideals above.
* `Rig.PerturbedPresentation.algEquivOfPerturbationEndomorphism`: the `A`-algebra isomorphism
  `B ≃ₐ[A] B'` of Stacks 0GAR, given the perturbation `σ` of the identity of `R` that the
  comparison map of Stacks 0GAQ gives rise to; the isomorphism is the one induced by `σ`.
* `Rig.PerturbedPresentation.exists_perturbationBound_algEquiv` and
  `Rig.PerturbedPresentation.exists_perturbationBound_algEquiv_completedPolynomial`: Stacks 0GAR,
  for a Noetherian `I`-adically complete `R` and for `R = A[x₁, …, x_r]^∧`, with the comparison
  perturbation `σ` as a hypothesis.

## Implementation notes

The endomorphism `σ` is a hypothesis here: it is the perturbation `xᵢ ↦ xᵢ + δᵢ` of the
identity of `A[x₁, …, x_r]^∧` through which the `A`-algebra map `φ : B → B'` factors, the
`δᵢ ∈ IⁿR` being lifts of `φ(xᵢ) - xᵢ ∈ IⁿB'`. Its construction uses adic completeness of `R ⧸ b`
(`Rig.isAdicComplete_quotient`) and the identification of `RestrictedPowerSeries.limitTopology`
with the adic topology of `I · R` (`RestrictedPowerSeries.isHomeomorph_adicCompletionEquiv`);
the theorem is accordingly stated at `Rig.completedPolynomialAlgebra` rather than at
`RestrictedPowerSeries`, the two being identified by
`RestrictedPowerSeries.adicCompletionAlgEquiv`.

Note that the surjectivity of the comparison map (Stacks 0315, part (1)) is used here only for
`σ` itself, not for `φ`: once the two ideals agree, both halves of the bijectivity of the induced
map come from `σ` being an automorphism.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GAR](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GAR)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00DV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00DV)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 07VE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07VE)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0315](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0315)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

universe u v

namespace Rig.PerturbedPresentation

section Endomorphism

variable {A : Type v} [CommRing A] {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [Algebra A R] (I : Ideal A)

/-- A linear functional on a finite free module is the combination of its values on the
standard basis vectors. -/
private theorem apply_eq_sum {m : ℕ} (g : (Fin m → R) →ₗ[R] R) (v : Fin m → R) :
    g v = ∑ i, v i * g (Pi.single i 1) := by
  have hv : v = ∑ i, v i • (Pi.single i 1 : Fin m → R) := by
    ext j
    simp [Finset.sum_apply, Pi.single_apply, Finset.sum_ite_eq]
  conv_lhs => rw [hv]
  rw [map_sum]
  simp [smul_eq_mul]

/-- The range of a map out of a finite free module, as an ideal, is generated by the images of
the standard basis vectors. -/
private theorem range_le_span {m : ℕ} (g : (Fin m → R) →ₗ[R] R) (T : Ideal R)
    (hT : ∀ i, g (Pi.single i 1) ∈ T) : (LinearMap.range g : Ideal R) ≤ T := by
  rintro x ⟨v, rfl⟩
  rw [apply_eq_sum g v]
  exact Ideal.sum_mem _ fun i _ => Ideal.mul_mem_left _ _ (hT i)

/-- An `A`-algebra endomorphism congruent to the identity modulo `IR` is congruent to it modulo
`I^{k+1}R` on `I^kR`: on a generator `r · a` with `a ∈ I^k` the difference is
`(σ r - r) · a`. -/
private theorem sub_mem_pow_succ (σ : R →ₐ[A] R)
    (hσ : ∀ x, σ x - x ∈ I.map (algebraMap A R)) (k : ℕ) {x : R}
    (hx : x ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ k) :
    σ x - x ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ (k + 1) := by
  have hpow : (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ k
      = Ideal.span ((algebraMap A R) '' ((I ^ k : Ideal A) : Set A)) := by
    rw [← Ideal.map_pow]
    rfl
  rw [hpow] at hx
  have key : ∀ y ∈ Ideal.span ((algebraMap A R) '' ((I ^ k : Ideal A) : Set A)),
      σ y - y ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ (k + 1) := by
    intro y hy
    induction hy using Submodule.span_induction with
    | mem z hz =>
      obtain ⟨a, -, rfl⟩ := hz
      rw [σ.commutes, sub_self]
      exact Submodule.zero_mem _
    | zero => simp
    | add z w hz hw ihz ihw =>
      rw [map_add, add_sub_add_comm]
      exact Submodule.add_mem _ ihz ihw
    | smul r z hz ih =>
      have hzk : z ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ k := by rw [hpow]; exact hz
      have h1 : σ r * (σ z - z) ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ (k + 1) :=
        Ideal.mul_mem_left _ _ ih
      have h2 : (σ r - r) * z ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ (k + 1) := by
        rw [pow_succ']
        exact Ideal.mul_mem_mul (hσ r) hzk
      have hrw : σ (r • z) - r • z = σ r * (σ z - z) + (σ r - r) * z := by
        rw [smul_eq_mul, map_mul]
        ring
      rw [hrw]
      exact Submodule.add_mem _ h1 h2
  exact key x hx

/-- An `A`-algebra endomorphism congruent to the identity is injective. If `σ` is an
`A`-algebra endomorphism of `R` with `σ x - x ∈ IR` for every `x`, and `R` is `IR`-Hausdorff,
then `σ` is injective: an element of the kernel lies in every power of `IR`, by induction on the
exponent using `sub_mem_pow_succ`. -/
public theorem injective_of_sub_mem (σ : R →ₐ[A] R)
    (hR : IsHausdorff (I.map (algebraMap A R)) R)
    (hσ : ∀ x, σ x - x ∈ I.map (algebraMap A R)) :
    Function.Injective σ := by
  rw [injective_iff_map_eq_zero σ]
  intro x hx
  refine hR.haus x fun k => ?_
  rw [SModEq.zero]
  have hmem : ∀ k : ℕ, x ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ k := by
    intro k
    induction k with
    | zero => simp
    | succ k ih =>
      have h := sub_mem_pow_succ I σ hσ k ih
      rw [show σ x = 0 from hx, zero_sub] at h
      simpa using Submodule.neg_mem _ h
  simpa using hmem k

/-- An `A`-algebra endomorphism congruent to the identity is an automorphism. Surjectivity is
Stacks 0315 part (1) — `σ` reduces to the identity modulo `IR` — and injectivity is
`injective_of_sub_mem`. -/
public theorem bijective_of_sub_mem (σ : R →ₐ[A] R)
    [hc : IsAdicComplete (I.map (algebraMap A R)) R]
    (hσ : ∀ x, σ x - x ∈ I.map (algebraMap A R)) :
    Function.Bijective σ := by
  refine ⟨injective_of_sub_mem I σ hc.toIsHausdorff hσ, ?_⟩
  refine surjective_of_surjective_mod I σ hc.toIsPrecomplete hc.toIsHausdorff ?_
  intro y
  obtain ⟨r, rfl⟩ := Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective (I := I.map (algebraMap A R)) y
  refine ⟨r, ?_⟩
  simpa using (Submodule.Quotient.eq _).mpr (hσ r)

end Endomorphism

section IdealEquality

variable {A : Type v} [CommRing A] {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [Algebra A R]
  (I : Ideal A)

/-- The equality of ideals that the final paragraph of Stacks 0GAR turns on. Let `c` work for
`g'` in the Artin-Rees lemma for `IR`, let `n ≥ c + 1`, and let `σ` be an endomorphism of `R`
carrying each `gᵢ` into the ideal `(g'₁, …, g'_m)` and with `σ(gᵢ) - g'ᵢ ∈ IⁿR`. Then
`σ((g₁, …, g_m)) R = (g'₁, …, g'_m)`.

One inclusion is the first hypothesis. For the other, the second hypothesis gives
`(g'₁, …, g'_m) ⊆ σ((g₁, …, g_m))R + IⁿR`, hence
`(g'₁, …, g'_m) ⊆ σ((g₁, …, g_m))R + ((g'₁, …, g'_m) ∩ IⁿR)`, and the Artin-Rees constant turns
the intersection into `I^{n-c} · (g'₁, …, g'_m) ⊆ I · (g'₁, …, g'_m)`. Nakayama (Stacks 00DV
part (2)) then collapses the difference, since `IR` lies in the Jacobson radical. -/
public theorem map_range_eq_range_of_artinRees
    (hJ : I.map (algebraMap A R) ≤ (⊥ : Ideal R).jacobson) {m c n : ℕ}
    {g g' : (Fin m → R) →ₗ[R] R} (σ : R →ₐ[A] R)
    (hc : IsArtinReesConstant (I.map (algebraMap A R)) g' c) (hn : c + 1 ≤ n)
    (hmem : ∀ i, σ (g (Pi.single i 1)) ∈ (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R))
    (hcongr : ∀ i, σ (g (Pi.single i 1)) - g' (Pi.single i 1)
      ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ n) :
    Ideal.map (σ : R →+* R) (LinearMap.range g : Ideal R)
      = (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R) := by
  set J : Ideal R := I.map (algebraMap A R) with hJdef
  set a : Ideal R := Ideal.map (σ : R →+* R) (LinearMap.range g : Ideal R) with hadef
  set b : Ideal R := (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R) with hbdef
  -- `a ≤ b`: the generators `σ(gᵢ)` of `a` lie in `b`.
  have hab : a ≤ b := by
    rw [hadef, Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap]
    refine range_le_span g _ fun i => ?_
    exact hmem i
  -- `b ≤ a ⊔ Jⁿ`: each `g'ᵢ` is `σ(gᵢ)` up to `Jⁿ`.
  have hsup : b ≤ a ⊔ J ^ n := by
    refine range_le_span g' _ fun i => ?_
    have h1 : σ (g (Pi.single i 1)) ∈ a :=
      Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ (LinearMap.mem_range_self g _)
    have h2 := Submodule.sub_mem (a ⊔ J ^ n) (Submodule.mem_sup_left h1)
      (Submodule.mem_sup_right (hcongr i))
    rwa [sub_sub_cancel] at h2
  -- Artin-Rees: `b ⊓ Jⁿ ≤ J • b`.
  have hAR : b ⊓ (J ^ n : Ideal R) ≤ J • b := by
    have h1 := hc n (by omega)
    have htop : (J ^ n) • (⊤ : Submodule R R) = (J ^ n : Ideal R) := by simp
    have htop' : (J ^ (n - c)) • (⊤ : Submodule R R) = (J ^ (n - c) : Ideal R) := by simp
    have hmapeq : Submodule.map g' ((J ^ (n - c)) • (⊤ : Submodule R (Fin m → R)))
        = (J ^ (n - c)) • b := by
      rw [Submodule.map_smul'', Submodule.map_top, hbdef]
    rw [htop] at h1
    refine h1.trans ?_
    rw [hmapeq]
    exact Submodule.smul_mono (Ideal.pow_le_self (by omega)) le_rfl
  -- `b ≤ a ⊔ J • b`.
  have hkey : b ≤ a ⊔ J • b := by
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨y, hy, z, hz, rfl⟩ := Submodule.mem_sup.mp (hsup hx)
    have hzb : z ∈ b := by
      have := Submodule.sub_mem b hx (hab hy)
      rwa [add_sub_cancel_left] at this
    exact Submodule.add_mem _ (Submodule.mem_sup_left hy)
      (Submodule.mem_sup_right (hAR ⟨hzb, hz⟩))
  -- Nakayama.
  have hfg : (b : Submodule R R).FG := IsNoetherian.noetherian _
  have hnak := Submodule.sup_eq_sup_smul_of_le_smul_of_le_jacobson (I := J) (J := ⊥)
    (N := (a : Submodule R R)) (N' := (b : Submodule R R)) hfg hJ hkey
  rw [Submodule.bot_smul, sup_bot_eq] at hnak
  exact hnak.symm.trans (sup_eq_right.mpr hab)

/-- The isomorphism `B ≃ₐ[A] B'` of Stacks 0GAR, given the perturbation of the identity.

Let `R` be a Noetherian `I`-adically complete `A`-algebra, let `c` work for `g'` in the Artin-Rees
lemma for `IR`, let `n ≥ c + 1`, and let `σ` be an `A`-algebra endomorphism of `R` congruent to
the identity modulo `IR` which carries each `gᵢ` into `(g'₁, …, g'_m)` and satisfies
`σ(gᵢ) - g'ᵢ ∈ IⁿR`. Then `σ` is an automorphism of `R` carrying `(g₁, …, g_m)` onto
`(g'₁, …, g'_m)`, hence induces an isomorphism of `A`-algebras

`B = R ⧸ (g₁, …, g_m) ≃ₐ[A] R ⧸ (g'₁, …, g'_m) = B'`.

The isomorphism *is* the map induced by `σ`; nothing is chosen. -/
public def algEquivOfPerturbationEndomorphism
    [IsAdicComplete (I.map (algebraMap A R)) R]
    (hJ : I.map (algebraMap A R) ≤ (⊥ : Ideal R).jacobson) {m c n : ℕ}
    {g g' : (Fin m → R) →ₗ[R] R} (σ : R →ₐ[A] R)
    (hσ : ∀ x, σ x - x ∈ I.map (algebraMap A R))
    (hc : IsArtinReesConstant (I.map (algebraMap A R)) g' c) (hn : c + 1 ≤ n)
    (hmem : ∀ i, σ (g (Pi.single i 1)) ∈ (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R))
    (hcongr : ∀ i, σ (g (Pi.single i 1)) - g' (Pi.single i 1)
      ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ n) :
    (R ⧸ (LinearMap.range g : Ideal R)) ≃ₐ[A] (R ⧸ (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R)) :=
  Ideal.quotientEquivAlg (LinearMap.range g : Ideal R) (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R)
    (AlgEquiv.ofBijective σ (bijective_of_sub_mem I σ hσ))
    (map_range_eq_range_of_artinRees I hJ σ hc hn hmem hcongr).symm

end IdealEquality

section Bound

variable {A : Type v} [CommRing A] {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [Algebra A R]
  (I : Ideal A)

/-- Stacks 0GAR, with the comparison perturbation carried.

Let `R` be a Noetherian `I`-adically complete `A`-algebra with `IR ≤ jacobson ⊥`, and let
`R^⊕t --K--> R^⊕m --g--> R → B → 0` be a resolution. Let `loss` be the precision loss of the
lifting lemma Stacks 0GAQ: in this library that lemma
(`Rig.exists_algHom_lift_of_dualSituation`) produces, from an `A`-algebra map
`B → B'/IⁿB'`, an `A`-algebra map `φ : B → B'` agreeing with it modulo `I^{n-loss}B'`, where
`loss = (2 + D)c` with `D = max(d, 1)`.

Then there is an integer `n₀` such that for every `n ≥ n₀` and every complex
`R^⊕t --K'--> R^⊕m --g'--> R` congruent to the given one modulo `IⁿR`:

* the perturbed complex is again a resolution (step (B) of the source proof), and
* every `A`-algebra endomorphism `σ` of `R` congruent to the identity modulo `I^{n-loss}R`
  which carries each `gᵢ` into `(g'₁, …, g'_m)` induces an isomorphism of `A`-algebras
  `B ≃ₐ[A] B'`.

The integer produced is `n₀ = c₁ + loss + 1`, where `c₁` is a common Artin-Rees constant for `K`
and `g`. This is where the coarser precision of Stacks 0GAQ and 0GAD is absorbed: `n` is
existentially quantified in Stacks 0GAR, so a conclusion reached only at level `n - loss` costs
nothing — one simply takes `n` larger by `loss`. The two uses of the bound are `c₁ + 1 ≤ n`, which
`artinRees_stability` (Stacks 07VE) needs for the perturbed complex to be exact and for `c₁` to
work for `g'` as well, and `c₁ + 1 ≤ n - loss`, which the Artin-Rees plus Nakayama argument of
`map_range_eq_range_of_artinRees` needs at the *reduced* precision at which `σ` is available. Both
follow from `n ≥ c₁ + loss + 1`. -/
public theorem exists_perturbationBound_algEquiv
    [IsAdicComplete (I.map (algebraMap A R)) R]
    (hJ : I.map (algebraMap A R) ≤ (⊥ : Ideal R).jacobson) (loss : ℕ)
    {t m : ℕ} (K : (Fin t → R) →ₗ[R] (Fin m → R)) (g : (Fin m → R) →ₗ[R] R)
    (hexact : LinearMap.ker g = LinearMap.range K) :
    ∃ n₀ : ℕ, ∀ n ≥ n₀, ∀ (K' : (Fin t → R) →ₗ[R] (Fin m → R)) (g' : (Fin m → R) →ₗ[R] R),
      g'.comp K' = 0 →
      CongruentModPow (I.map (algebraMap A R)) K K' n →
      CongruentModPow (I.map (algebraMap A R)) g g' n →
        LinearMap.ker g' = LinearMap.range K' ∧
        ∀ σ : R →ₐ[A] R,
          (∀ x, σ x - x ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ (n - loss)) →
          (∀ i, σ (g (Pi.single i 1)) ∈ (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R)) →
          Nonempty ((R ⧸ (LinearMap.range g : Ideal R)) ≃ₐ[A]
            (R ⧸ (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal R))) := by
  obtain ⟨c, hK, hg⟩ := exists_isArtinReesConstant_pair (I.map (algebraMap A R)) K g
  refine ⟨c + loss + 1, fun n hn K' g' hcomplex hKK' hgg' => ?_⟩
  have hn1 : c + 1 ≤ n := by omega
  have hstab := artinRees_stability (I.map (algebraMap A R)) hJ K K' g g' c hcomplex hK hg hexact
    (hKK'.mono hn1) (hgg'.mono hn1)
  refine ⟨hstab.2, fun σ hσ hmem => ⟨?_⟩⟩
  have hnl : c + 1 ≤ n - loss := by omega
  refine algEquivOfPerturbationEndomorphism I hJ σ ?_ hstab.1 hnl hmem ?_
  · intro x
    exact Ideal.pow_le_self (by omega) (hσ x)
  · intro i
    have hd : g (Pi.single i 1) - g' (Pi.single i 1)
        ∈ ((I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ n : Ideal R) := by
      have h := hgg' (LinearMap.mem_range_self (g - g') (Pi.single i 1))
      rw [LinearMap.sub_apply] at h
      simpa using h
    have hsum := Submodule.add_mem ((I.map (algebraMap A R)) ^ (n - loss))
      (hσ (g (Pi.single i 1)))
      (Ideal.pow_le_pow_right (by omega : n - loss ≤ n) hd)
    rwa [sub_add_sub_cancel] at hsum

end Bound

section CompletedPolynomial

variable (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A] (I : Ideal A) (r : ℕ)

/-- `P = A[x₁, …, x_r]^∧` is complete for the ideal `IP`: the ideal `I A[x]` is finitely
generated, so the adic completion is complete for the ideal it generates. -/
public theorem isAdicComplete_completedPolynomialAlgebra :
    IsAdicComplete (I.map (algebraMap A (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r)))
      (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r) := by
  have hmap :
      (I.map (MvPolynomial.C : A →+* MvPolynomial (Fin r) A)).map
          (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin r) A) (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))
        = I.map (algebraMap A (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r)) := by
    rw [Ideal.map_map, algebraMap_comp_C]
  rw [← hmap]
  exact AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_self _
    (Ideal.fg_of_isNoetherianRing (I.map (MvPolynomial.C : A →+* MvPolynomial (Fin r) A)))

/-- Stacks 0GAR over `P = A[x₁, …, x_r]^∧`, with the comparison perturbation carried.

This is `exists_perturbationBound_algEquiv` with its two ring-level hypotheses discharged for the
ring `P` that Stacks 0GAR is about: `P` is Noetherian by Stacks 0316, it is `IP`-adically
complete by `isAdicComplete_completedPolynomialAlgebra`, and `IP ≤ jacobson ⊥` by
`Rig.PerturbedPresentation.map_algebraMap_le_jacobson_bot` (Stacks 05GI). -/
public theorem exists_perturbationBound_algEquiv_completedPolynomial (loss : ℕ) {t m : ℕ}
    (K : (Fin t → completedPolynomialAlgebra I r) →ₗ[completedPolynomialAlgebra I r]
      (Fin m → completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))
    (g : (Fin m → completedPolynomialAlgebra I r) →ₗ[completedPolynomialAlgebra I r]
      completedPolynomialAlgebra I r)
    (hexact : LinearMap.ker g = LinearMap.range K) :
    ∃ n₀ : ℕ, ∀ n ≥ n₀,
      ∀ (K' : (Fin t → completedPolynomialAlgebra I r) →ₗ[completedPolynomialAlgebra I r]
          (Fin m → completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))
        (g' : (Fin m → completedPolynomialAlgebra I r) →ₗ[completedPolynomialAlgebra I r]
          completedPolynomialAlgebra I r),
      g'.comp K' = 0 →
      CongruentModPow (I.map (algebraMap A (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))) K K' n →
      CongruentModPow (I.map (algebraMap A (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))) g g' n →
        LinearMap.ker g' = LinearMap.range K' ∧
        ∀ σ : completedPolynomialAlgebra I r →ₐ[A] completedPolynomialAlgebra I r,
          (∀ x, σ x - x
            ∈ (I.map (algebraMap A (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))) ^ (n - loss)) →
          (∀ i, σ (g (Pi.single i 1))
            ∈ (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))) →
          Nonempty
            ((completedPolynomialAlgebra I r ⧸
                (LinearMap.range g : Ideal (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r))) ≃ₐ[A]
              (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r ⧸
                (LinearMap.range g' : Ideal (completedPolynomialAlgebra I r)))) :=
  let := isAdicComplete_completedPolynomialAlgebra A I r
